## New recurring-pattern lessons surfaced during Batch 18 Item 1 (apply at next batch PRE-FLIGHT):

- 🟢 PURE-REDUCER-FROM-PROVIDER PATTERN: extract pure reducer to its own non-"use client"
  module so vitest in node environment can fully test it. Provider becomes a thin "use client"
  wrapper around useReducer. Reusable for ALL future client-state features (e.g. multi-step
  form wizard state, draft autosave state, undo/redo stack).

- 🟢 PER-TENANT LOCALSTORAGE KEY PATTERN: `orqafy-${feature}-${tenantSlug}` prevents cross-
  tenant data bleed on shared devices. CartProvider takes tenantSlug as prop from the layout's
  params Promise. Same pattern applies to any future tenant-scoped client state.

- 🟢 SSR HYDRATION SAFETY VIA HYDRATED FLAG: useState(false) → useEffect reads storage →
  setHydrated(true). UI shows empty state until hydrated; writes suppressed until hydrated.
  Click handlers disabled until hydrated. Prevents both classic Next.js hydration mismatch
  AND the lost-click bug from interacting before mount completes.

- 🔴 GOTCHA REPEAT: `@next/next/no-img-element` disable comment is a foot-gun in this project
  (no @next/next ESLint plugin loaded). 2nd occurrence (Batch 15 Item 3 was first).
  NEVER add this disable comment — native <img alt=...> is sufficient and lint-clean.

- 🟢 STRUCTURAL NARROWING REMINDER: after `"X" in obj && typeof obj === "object"`,
  TypeScript narrows `obj` to `{ X: unknown } & object` automatically. Explicit
  `(obj as { X: unknown })` casts are redundant and tripped by @typescript-eslint/no-unnecessary-type-assertion.

- 🟢 INTENTIONAL SCOPE-TRIM PRECEDENT: Batch 18 Item 1 deliberately shipped only half of the
  proposed Direction A bundle (cart only, not cart+checkout). First time the batch series
  has intentionally trimmed scope below the proposed Plan. Rationale was: Item 2 introduces
  new attack surface (first publicProcedure on storefront) deserving its own focused review.
  Pattern: when a Direction bundles N items and one item introduces a NEW security/auth
  surface, prefer to ship that item in its own batch even if total token count fits in one
  session. Optimizes for review focus, not throughput.

